Z||000000||gtsonly||11-04-2001||09:41 AM||||So, here I am, about a month and a half into a top end rebuild of my GT. It only had 83,000 miles on it and most if not all valve seals were bad. So I pulled the whole top end and now I am porting and polishing Intake, Exhaust and anything else I can remove metal from. Exhaust manifolds suck!!!! I got the welds re-enforced and started removing the restrictions. Gladly trade some a set of stock manifolds for a set of headers.  As it goes, I hope to have the car back on the road by December with a new top end. Wish me luck<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero001_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000001||avengador1||11-04-2001||02:19 PM||||While you have the top half out, why not get the throttle body and the intake manifold bored out. You could also get the intake manifold extrude honed and match all the port openings. If you're gonna let more air out you might as well let more in. All of this could be done by yourself to save money. You could also remove the restrive air baffle, between the intake grill and air filter housing, and fab up a tube to replace it with some pvc pipping. You could even put on a free flow cat. Lots of good luck and keep up the good work. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtsonly||11-04-2001||04:02 PM||||I did do the intake manifold to the heads. Mind you I am no pro!!!!  I am not going to worry about the fresh air intake, I want the engine stock. Still looking for a set of headers. Anyone????<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero001_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000003||fastback87||11-05-2001||12:25 PM||||The fresh air intake isn't on the engine it is part of the body that lets fresh outside air into the filter, then it goes through that rubber tube and into the throttle body.  Not to sound like a jerk but I think you mean you want the engine "stock like".  Stock engines don't come ported or with headers.  <P>That is the same thing I want to do with my spare engine when I start rebuilding it, except I want to see how much possible HP and torque I can get from the 2.8 with all the fiero intake stuff.  I will change heads if i can and port everything, even the heater vents if that will help....I don't think it will though :-)||||reg||1
Z||000004||gtsonly||11-05-2001||12:30 PM||||I am aware of where the fresh air intake is, the post prior to was talking about modifying the tube to make a more direct flow. I want the engine stock looking. Your right that stock does not mean porting and polishing but what you can't see............<BR>The engine looks stock!!!!!!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero001_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000005||Voytek||11-05-2001||01:16 PM||||Hey<BR>How are you porting and polishing the intakes?  I've been thinking about doing this too.  Are you doing the upper plenum, the middle and lower intake?  Are you doing this yourself?<BR>Thx<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||gtsonly||11-05-2001||02:28 PM||||For right now, I am only doing the exhaust manifolds to the head and the lower intake manifold to the head. Does that make sense???<BR>I took a new gasket, lined it up and traced with a marker the outline of the opening. Basically I am matching the gasket to the port. Lots of grinding later and some finish sanding, wahhhhlaaaaaa!!!!   Mind you as I said, this is my first attempt at this.  Well see!!!!!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero001_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000007||avengador1||11-05-2001||08:42 PM||||I'm going to paraphrase an article from "The Big Guide To Fiero Kits and Parts". An engine can be seen as an air pump. The more air it pumps, the more power it develops. In order to increase airflow, any obstructions must be removed. The most obvious restriction is the exhaust system.Replace the muffler and catalytic converter with high flow units. Headers may be installed (not a really big advantage). Next look at the intake system. The easiest improvement is to replace the air filter with a K&N filter. Next go to the inside of the engine. Port matching, do this by removing all three intake sections, to not get dirt or metal in the engine. Use the gaskets as a template to open the holes to the intake and the lower intake ports on the heads. The heads should be off the engine and all of the valvetrain should be removed. when done all parts should be thoroughly rinsed and cleaned. The exhaust manifolds have obstructions in the # 1,3,2, and #4 cylinder exhaust ports. These block 10 to 15% of the exhaust port area. Don't remove more than the obstructions because the tubes are thin walled.Don't port match or you will cut into the welds that hold them together. Clean out real good to not get metal in the catalytic converter. This cleanup is supposed to be good for 6 to 10 H.P. The best headers are only good for 12 to 15 H.P. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mickey_Moose||11-05-2001||09:07 PM||||You can port match the exhaust, but you FIRST have to get them welded on the outside of the manifolds.<P>Voytek &gt;&gt; easy to do, the main tool you will need is a good die grinder - then it's just a matter of putting the time in to do it. Did this myself early this spring, not sure how much of a gain it was, but it was noticable. I have this little patch of raod that I test the car on, same distance: before I could get the car up to almost 60 mph...after, just over 70 mph. I also had the exhaust ceramic coated (cost was about $100 here in Edmonton).<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||10
